{
  "caseId": "averitec-dev-0419",
  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention fraudulently add deaths from poisoning, trauma, and unintentional injury to their tally of COVID-19 deaths.",
      "sourceText": "The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention fraudulently add deaths from poisoning, trauma, and unintentional injury to their tally of COVID-19 deaths.",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 159
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-5dfdb3a51082::sentence-5",
          "documentId": "doc-5dfdb3a51082",
          "text": "On April 3, 2020, the CDC's National Center for Health Statistics issued guidance to physicians, coroners, and medical examiners who fill out death certificates to list COVID-19 as an underlying cause of death in the absence of a test \"if the circumstances are compelling within a reasonable degree of certainty.\"\n",
          "start": 843,
          "end": 1157
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-efeac11ee951::sentence-31",
          "documentId": "doc-efeac11ee951",
          "text": "See the CDC Guidance on Certifying COVID-19 Deaths (PDF).\n",
          "start": 3829,
          "end": 3887
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-efeac11ee951::sentence-16",
          "documentId": "doc-efeac11ee951",
          "text": "If a Coroner suspects COVID-19 is the cause of death, should COVID-19 be listed as the cause of death without laboratory test confirmation?\n",
          "start": 1974,
          "end": 2114
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-efeac11ee951::sentence-15",
          "documentId": "doc-efeac11ee951",
          "text": "Yes, a Medical Examiner may conclude the cause of death is COVID-19 and list it in Part I of the death certificate.\n",
          "start": 1858,
          "end": 1974
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-5dfdb3a51082::sentence-17",
          "documentId": "doc-5dfdb3a51082",
          "text": "As of this writing, the U.S. had more than 700,000 COVID-19 cases, with the death count nearing 40,000. ",
          "start": 3257,
          "end": 3361
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-5dfdb3a51082::sentence-7",
          "documentId": "doc-5dfdb3a51082",
          "text": "In cases where a definite diagnosis of COVID–19 cannot be made, but it is suspected or likely (e.g., the circumstances are compelling within a reasonable degree of certainty), it is acceptable to report COVID–19 on a death certificate as 'probable' or 'presumed.' ",
          "start": 1181,
          "end": 1445
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
